<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # All About My Body – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> All About My Body</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Hats and Shoes!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write simple responses to texts. Interactive: Write simple words supported with drawings to peers.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write simple sentence responses to questions about body parts and clothing (e.g., What do you wear on your head? "I wear a hat."). Can write simple words about body parts supported with drawings to communicate ideas (e.g., drawing a hand and writing "hand" to label it). Can write, with support, simple phrases or sentences about personal experiences with body parts or clothing (e.g., "I wear my raincoat on rainy days.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="background-color: #e0e0e0; border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and starts a "Body Part Chant" while pointing to body parts and clothing. "Head, shoulders, knees and toes, knees and toes! Hat, shoes, coat and socks, coat and socks!" The teacher then shows flashcards of body parts (head, hand, foot) and clothing (hat, shoes, socks) and asks students to quickly point and say the word. Then, students are asked to write one word they remember on a mini-whiteboard or a piece of scratch paper (e.g., "hat" or "hand").</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to write simple words. The teacher displays large picture cards of a 'hat' and a 'hand' on the board. The teacher says "hat" and slowly writes the word "hat" on the board, pointing to each letter and emphasizing the /h/ sound. "H-A-T, hat." Then, the teacher does the same for "hand", emphasizing the /h/ sound and showing how to form the letters. The teacher then models writing the sentence "This is my hat." and "This is my hand." on the board.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is this word?" (Pointing to 'hat'). Expected answer: "Hat!"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you write 'hat' like me?" (Teacher shows the word again). Expected action: Students try to write.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "All About My Body".</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple illustrated short text or poem (e.g., a "getting dressed" story) projected on the board or on a worksheet, featuring body parts and clothing vocabulary. The text will contain sentences like: "This is my head. I wear a hat on my head. These are my feet. I wear shoes on my feet. My shoes are clean."</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Identifying body parts and clothing; Describing and identifying body parts and clothing (e.g., "This is my hand."); Writing simple responses to text.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Writing focus):</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads the short text aloud, pointing to the words and pictures.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are given a worksheet with the text and some blanks to fill in with missing vocabulary words from a word bank (e.g., "I wear a ____ on my head." Word bank: hat, shoes, socks).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students match pictures of body parts/clothing to their corresponding written words by drawing a line, then copy the words next to the pictures.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks simple WH-questions orally (e.g., "What do I wear on my head?"). Students write the answer "A hat" or "Hat." on their worksheet.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Writing simple words and sentences about body parts and clothing, using Present Simple ("This is...", "I wear...") and demonstratives ("This", "These"), possessive adjectives ("my"), and prepositions ("on"). Focus on vocabulary: head, hand, foot, hat, shoes, socks, coat, clean, dirty, warm, cold.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
            <ol>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Fill-in-the-Blanks:</b> Students receive a worksheet with incomplete sentences and pictures. For example, a picture of a hand with "This is my __nd." and a picture of a hat with "I wear a h__t." Students fill in the missing letters to complete the words. The teacher monitors and helps with spelling and letter formation.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Sentence Builder:</b> The teacher provides sentence frames on the board or a worksheet: "This is my _______." and "I wear my _______ on my _______." Students choose words from a provided list (e.g., head, foot, arm, hat, shoes, socks, hand) to complete the sentences. For example, they might write: "This is my arm." "I wear my socks on my feet." Teacher emphasizes correct capitalization and punctuation.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Interactive Writing - Draw and Label:</b> Students work in pairs. Each student draws two body parts or pieces of clothing they learned (e.g., an eye, a knee, a jacket, pants) on a piece of paper. Underneath each drawing, they write the corresponding word or a simple sentence like "This is my eye." They then exchange their drawings with their partner. The partner checks the writing for legibility and attempts to add one more descriptive word (e.g., "clean socks" or "warm jacket") if they can, or circle a letter that needs correction with teacher support.</li>
            </ol>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "All About My Body".</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual Written Product - "My Clothes and Body Book Page".</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will create a page for their "My Clothes and Body Book." Each student receives a blank sheet of paper divided into two sections. In the first section, they draw a picture of themselves wearing a specific piece of clothing (e.g., a hat or shoes). Below the drawing, they write a simple sentence responding to the question "What do you wear on your head?" (e.g., "I wear a hat.") or "What do you wear on your feet?" (e.g., "I wear shoes."). In the second section, they draw another body part (e.g., a hand or an ear) and write a label or a simple sentence using demonstratives (e.g., "This is my hand." or "My hand is clean."). They can also add an adjective like "My hat is warm." or "My shoes are dirty." The teacher circulates, offering support with spelling and sentence structure.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their "My Clothes and Body Book Page." The teacher collects the pages and uses a simple checklist to assess students' writing.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Used target vocabulary (at least 3 words: e.g., hat, head, hand, shoes, feet).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Wrote simple sentences (e.g., "I wear a hat," "This is my hand").</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Responses to questions are clear (e.g., "I wear a hat." for "What do you wear on your head?").</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Drawings support written words/sentences.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Legibility of writing.</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences on a small paper slip before leaving:</p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today I wrote about __________."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can write the word __________."</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words did I write today that I learned?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy or difficult about writing today?"</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Most students successfully wrote simple words and sentences about body parts and clothing. The drawing and labeling activity was very engaging, and students enjoyed creating their book pages. Some students struggled with letter formation for less common vocabulary words (e.g., 'elbow', 'shoulder') but were able to write the core vocabulary. I need to continue providing ample visual support and sentence frames for writing tasks. For the next lesson, I will ensure more opportunities for peer interaction in checking written work and consolidating ideas through a project.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on producing written sentences and words. The next lesson (Lesson 5: Mediation & Integration) will consolidate all the learned vocabulary and grammar through a collaborative project, where students will explain their written work and ideas to peers and a wider audience.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>